<GuidePage
	title="What Does Your eGFR Number Mean? eGFR 90 to 15 Explained"
	description="What an eGFR of 90, 70, 60, 55, 50, 45, 30, or 15 means, which CKD stage it falls in, whether one low result is a concern, and what to ask your doctor next."
	h1="What does your eGFR number mean?"
	path="/egfr-results"
	crumb="eGFR Results Explained"
	kind="article"
	modified="2026-11-03"
	related={['/', '/ckd-stages', '/normal-gfr-by-age', '/how-to-improve-gfr']}
>
	<Fragment slot="intro">
		<p>
			eGFR estimates how well your kidneys filter blood, and higher is better. A value of 90 or above
			is normal for most adults. A value below 60 that lasts more than three months meets the
			definition of chronic kidney disease (CKD). One result on its own does not diagnose anything,
			because eGFR moves between tests. Find your number below, or
			<a href="/">calculate your eGFR</a> if you only have a creatinine result.
		</p>
	</Fragment>

	<h2>eGFR results at a glance</h2>
	<div class="table-wrap">
		<table>
			<caption class="sr-only">eGFR ranges and what they indicate, in mL/min/1.73m²</caption>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col">eGFR</th><th scope="col">CKD stage</th><th scope="col">Kidney function</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><th scope="row">90 or above</th><td>G1</td><td>Normal or high</td></tr>
				<tr><th scope="row">60&ndash;89</th><td>G2</td><td>Mildly decreased</td></tr>
				<tr><th scope="row">45&ndash;59</th><td>G3a</td><td>Mildly to moderately decreased</td></tr>
				<tr><th scope="row">30&ndash;44</th><td>G3b</td><td>Moderately to severely decreased</td></tr>
				<tr><th scope="row">15&ndash;29</th><td>G4</td><td>Severely decreased</td></tr>
				<tr><th scope="row">Below 15</th><td>G5</td><td>Kidney failure</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>

	<h2>eGFR of 90 or higher</h2>
	<p>
		This is in the normal range. It counts as stage 1 CKD only if there is also a sign of kidney
		damage, such as protein in the urine. Without one, it does not mean CKD.
	</p>

	<h2>eGFR of 70</h2>
	<p>
		An eGFR of 70, like anything from 60 to 89, is in category G2. It is common as people get older:
		the average eGFR for people aged 70 and over is about 75. It only counts as CKD if a marker of
		kidney damage is also present, so a urine albumin test is the useful next step. See
		<a href="/normal-gfr-by-age">normal GFR by age</a> for how your number compares.
	</p>

	<h2>eGFR of 60</h2>
	<p>
		60 sits right on the line. It is the bottom of the mildly decreased range, and anything below it
		for more than three months meets the definition of CKD. If yours is around 60, ask for a repeat test
		and a urine albumin test so you can see whether it is stable.
	</p>

	<h2>eGFR of 55 or 50</h2>
	<p>
		These fall in category G3a, also called stage 3a CKD if they last three months or more. Many people
		at this level feel fine. Care usually focuses on blood pressure, blood sugar, and a review of
		medicines, including over-the-counter painkillers.
	</p>

	<h2>eGFR of 45</h2>
	<p>
		45 is the lowest value in G3a. Just below it, 30 to 44 is G3b. Clinicians often check for
		complications such as anemia and bone-mineral changes at this level, and some medicine doses may
		need to change.
	</p>

	<h2>eGFR of 30</h2>
	<p>
		30 is the lowest value in G3b, and below it is G4. An eGFR under 30 is a common point for referral
		to a nephrologist, who can plan ahead for treatment options if function keeps falling.
	</p>

	<h2>eGFR of 15 or below</h2>
	<p>
		An eGFR of exactly 15 is the bottom of G4. Below 15 is category G5, kidney failure. Many people at this level need dialysis or a transplant,
		and some choose conservative care. This needs care from a kidney specialist.
	</p>

	<h2>Why one low result may not mean CKD</h2>
	<p>Some things can lower your eGFR without long-term kidney damage:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Eating a lot of meat before the test</li>
		<li>Creatine supplements</li>
		<li>Intense exercise right before the test</li>
		<li>Dehydration</li>
		<li>Having a lot of muscle</li>
		<li>Certain medicines</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		A sudden drop can also come from acute kidney injury, which usually improves once the cause is
		treated. That is why clinicians look at the trend over at least three months.
	</p>

	<h2>What to ask your doctor</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Is this a new drop, or has my eGFR been stable?</li>
		<li>Should I repeat the test, and do I need a urine albumin test?</li>
		<li>Would a cystatin C test give a more accurate picture for me?</li>
		<li>Do any of my medicines or supplements need to change?</li>
		<li>Do I need to see a nephrologist?</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Get prompt medical advice if a low result comes with reduced urination, swelling, confusion, or
		shortness of breath. For ways to protect your kidneys, see
		<a href="/how-to-improve-gfr">how to improve GFR</a>.
	</p>

